;;; RFC 3986 conformance audit -- the DIFFERENTIAL adversarial corpus.
;;;
;;; AUTHORED, NOT EXTRACTED. Like test/conformance/charset/ and test/conformance/
;;; host/ (and unlike the derived test/conformance/rfc3986/), this file is
;;; HAND-DERIVED from the ABNF (test/conformance/rfc3986/rfc3986.abnf) -- the RFC
;;; lists no invalid URIs. It lives in its own dir, apart from the corpus-
;;; provenance-guarded rfc3986/, and is gated by being run against BOTH the product
;;; (uri-parse-validate) and the independent oracle ((uri test abnf-oracle)).
;;;
;;; WHY IT EXISTS. The exhaustive sweep in abnf-differential-tests.sps covers every
;;; SHORT string over a reduced alphabet completely, but the length bound cannot
;;; reach the DEEP, STRUCTURED inputs where a divergence is most likely to hide: a
;;; full IPv6 literal, an IPv4 octet on its boundary, a long pct-encoded run, a
;;; multi-segment path whose colon lands in a later segment, an empty component in
;;; combination. These are those inputs.
;;;
;;; THE THIRD READING. Each row carries its ABNF-correct verdict, so the lane checks
;;; the oracle AND the product against a hand reading of Appendix A -- a shared
;;; oracle/product blind spot still reddens against it. Get
;;; a verdict wrong here and the row reddens on whichever of the two the ABNF agrees
;;; with; that is the row doing its job, not a bug in the harness.
;;;
;;; A row is (mode input expect note):
;;;   mode    uri / relative-ref / uri-reference -- the production to recognize
;;;   input   the bytes -- a STRING (its UTF-8 bytes) or a #vu8(...) bytevector
;;;   expect  accept | reject -- the ABNF verdict for `input` in `mode`
;;;   note    the ABNF justification, short
(cases
